@@ -1184,6 +1184,39 @@ These limits are approximate, so `vmagent` can underflow or overflow them by a s
 
 See also [cardinality explorer docs](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/single-server-victoriametrics/#cardinality-explorer).
 
+## Limiting the number of scrape targets per job
+
+By default, `vmagent` scrapes every target discovered for a `job_name`, regardless of which
+[service discovery mechanism](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/) produced it.
+This is usually fine, but a misconfigured discovery source (for example, an accidentally removed tag
+filter on a Kubernetes, Consul or cloud API based service discovery, or an overly broad catalog query)
+can suddenly return a huge number of targets for a single job. Scraping all of them at once can spike
+`vmagent`'s memory and CPU usage and degrade scraping for every other job running on the same instance.
+
+The `max_targets` option acts as a safety net for this:
+
+* Via `-promscrape.maxTargetsPerJob` command-line flag. This sets the default limit for all jobs
+  defined in the file pointed to by `-promscrape.config`.
+* Via `max_targets` config option at [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs) section.
+  The `max_targets` option overrides `-promscrape.maxTargetsPerJob` on a per-`scrape_config` (per `job_name`) basis.
+
+By default (`max_targets` unset or `<= 0`) there is no limit, so existing setups are unaffected.
+
+Once a job's discovered target count exceeds its `max_targets`, `vmagent` keeps scraping only up to
+`max_targets` of them; the rest are dropped instead of crashing `vmagent`, rejecting the whole job,
+or silently scraping everything. The limit is applied uniformly no matter which service discovery
+mechanism(s) supplied the targets for the job, and no matter how many different mechanisms are
+combined within a single `scrape_config`.
+
+Jobs currently over their `max_targets` limit are easy to spot from the same places used for checking
+scrape target health:
+
+* The [`/targets`](#how-to-collect-metrics-in-prometheus-format) page shows, next to the job's up/total
+  target count, how many of its targets are currently excluded because the limit was reached.
+* The excluded targets themselves show up as dropped targets, labeled with the
+  `max_targets limit reached` reason, on the `/service-discovery` page, and as `droppedTargets`
+  entries in the `/api/v1/targets` JSON API, just like targets dropped by relabeling.
+
 ## Monitoring
 
 `vmagent` exports various metrics in Prometheus exposition format at `http://vmagent-host:8429/metrics` page.
